WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.459
Hello and welcome to this course on

2
00:00:03.459 --> 00:00:07.099
AI Systems Introduction to Navigation.

3
00:00:07.099 --> 00:00:11.980
We'll be doing this course in Unreal Engine version 5.5.

4
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.859
However, most of this

5
00:00:14.859 --> 00:00:18.300
hasn't changed for quite a few versions of the engine,

6
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.780
so you should be okay working in

7
00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:26.109
previous engine versions I think all the way through five.

8
00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:27.670
Now in this, we're going to go over

9
00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:29.710
a number of different things.

10
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:32.649
We're going to go over how to

11
00:00:32.649 --> 00:00:36.310
set up navigation and something called a NavMesh.

12
00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:40.750
We're going to look at methods of customizing it so that we

13
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.990
can make the behavior

14
00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:46.890
respond to the environment that we've created.

15
00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:51.070
We're also going to have a go at making it dynamic

16
00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.710
so that it changes at runtime

17
00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.890
without having too much of a cost.

18
00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:56.890
Finally, we're going to look at

19
00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:01.950
different methods of getting the AI to avoid each other,

20
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:04.900
so they don't all get clumped up.

21
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.340
The first question, what is a NavMesh?

22
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.750
Well, a NavMesh or more common full name of

23
00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:14.790
a navigation mesh is

24
00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:19.769
a simplified map of the walkable areas used in game.

25
00:01:19.769 --> 00:01:24.390
What it is, is it is a map which is generated and

26
00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:28.590
created and modifiers are added to it,

27
00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.390
and it is laid over the world.

28
00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:35.129
Its primary function is just so that we can

29
00:01:35.129 --> 00:01:40.189
figure out a path of how to get from one place to another.

30
00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:44.579
It shows us what the walkable areas are.

31
00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:46.500
This is a standard method,

32
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.140
it pre-calculates pathfinding,

33
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.319
allowing the AI to navigate complex environments efficiently.

34
00:01:53.319 --> 00:01:58.040
All that means is it will break down all of the environment into

35
00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.680
blocks and calculate the distance

36
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.439
that will be required to move across them.

37
00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:08.039
Therefore, rather than actually having

38
00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:11.719
to analyze the environment every single time that it goes in,

39
00:02:11.719 --> 00:02:15.919
we just need to run an algorithm against

40
00:02:15.919 --> 00:02:20.599
the table generated blocks and it'll tell us

41
00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:25.800
the most efficient way to move through the environment.

42
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.400
Now, it's not entirely realistic.

43
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.360
Take for example, we had a maze.

44
00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.280
Using this method, the AI is going to know

45
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:39.240
the exact best most efficient path to get through that maze,

46
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:44.759
to navigate it, where a natural AI would probably get lost

47
00:02:44.759 --> 00:02:50.759
and would end up having to explore to find its way through it.

48
00:02:50.759 --> 00:02:53.000
But a NavMesh is always going to give us

49
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.599
the most efficient best path.

50
00:02:55.599 --> 00:03:00.560
Now, if you want to see some working examples

51
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.740
of a NavMesh in AI movement setup,

52
00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.639
I can recommend two different projects to you.

53
00:03:07.639 --> 00:03:11.199
The first one is the Lyra example game.

54
00:03:11.199 --> 00:03:17.520
This is a fully implemented AAA game,

55
00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.879
set up so that you can explore and see how everything is created.

56
00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:24.039
In this, we do rely on the AI,

57
00:03:24.039 --> 00:03:27.599
so you can see the AI set up there.

58
00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:32.240
Secondly, we have something called the Matrix City demo.

59
00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:38.240
Now, this is a massive open-world city,

60
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.319
which has hundreds and hundreds of

61
00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:44.840
different AIs wandering around it with logic built-in.

62
00:03:44.919 --> 00:03:53.639
Now, this also uses our mass crowd system to do avoidance.

63
00:03:53.639 --> 00:03:58.319
So this is a good example of that setup.

64
00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:00.560
For this project, when we get into it,

65
00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.000
we do not require you to download a project for this.

66
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.159
We are going to be using the top-down template,

67
00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:11.919
which can be found under the game section.

68
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:17.040
We'll be using that to do all of our examples.

69
00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.680
So I recommend that you follow along with this.

70
00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.079
I recommend that you open up that top-down template,

71
00:04:24.079 --> 00:04:26.079
create a new project for that,

72
00:04:26.079 --> 00:04:28.199
and we'll be able to follow

73
00:04:28.199 --> 00:04:30.480
along with everything that we're doing.

74
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.360
Thank you very much.

75
00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.800
In the next videos, we will jump into the browser.
